While detailed finite-sample properties are well understood in many settings for non-differentiable methods, including the linear Gaussian (Shojaie and Michailidis, 2010; van de Geer and Bühlmann, 2013; Gao et al., 2022; Xu et al., 2025a), linear non-Gaussian (Loh and Bühlmann, 2014), and non-linear (Park and Raskutti, 2018; Park and Park, 2019b; Wang et al., 2024) settings, very little is known for differentiable approaches based on continuous optimization. At the same time, differentiable methods (Zheng et al., 2018; Yu et al., 2019; Brouillard et al., 2020) are often used in practice due to their ease of implementation and convenient integration into existing differentiable machine learning (ML) pipelines. Despite progress on both fronts, explicit finite-sample theory for differentiable formulations remains comparatively limited, especially beyond linear Gaussian models.

We study differentiable causal structure learning for DAGs with GLM conditional distributions, allowing nonlinear relationships. To this aim, we first cast causal structure learning as an estimation problem with a single global objective over all topological orders, where the objective is defined for a broad class of generalized linear models (GLMs) over a class of continuous DAG coefficient matrices. We refer to these as GLM-DAGs in the sequel. The GLM family is chosen as it is both general enough to permit nonlinear relationships, while also being concrete and explicit. Under this general framework, we give conditions under which the global minimizers of the objective are identifiable, and establish non-asymptotic finite-sample guarantees, improving both the scope and reliability
